// Building strings, end to end, and releasing each one.
//
// Two shapes, because they exercise different release points:
//
// 1. An accumulator rebound in a loop. Every `acc = acc + "x"` builds a new
//    string, secures its reference, and *then* releases the previous one, so
//    the program holds one string at a time rather than one per iteration.
// 2. A fresh string each iteration. The loop body is a scope, so the binding
//    gives its reference back when the iteration ends.
//
// The leak test in `leaks.rs` runs this with `YASHY_LEAK_REPORT=1` and asserts
// the report says zero: the printed lengths prove the values are right, and
// the report proves the references came back.
fn main() {
    let acc = "";
    let i = 0;
    while i < 100 {
        acc = acc + "x";
        i += 1;
    }
    print(acc.len());
    println("");

    let last = 0;
    i = 0;
    while i < 100 {
        let fresh = "item " + str_from_i64(i);
        last = fresh.len();
        i += 1;
    }
    print(last);
    println("");
}
